{"cuisine/yup-ik-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/yup-ik-cuisine","n":"Yup'ik cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Alaska Native foodways","cuisine/alaska-native-foodways"]],"sub":"Indigenous foodways, Yukon-Kuskokwim Delta and Bristol Bay, Alaska","names":"Yup'ik, Cup'ik","intro":[["Yup'ik and Cup'ik people live along Alaska's southwest coast and up the Yukon and Kuskokwim rivers. Summer is fish camp, when ",["Salmon","ingredient/salmon"]," is cut, dried and smoked to last the winter, and fall is for berries and greens from the tundra."],[["Akutaq","dish/akutaq"],", from a Yup'ik word for \"mixed together,\" is whipped fat with berries and sometimes fish. Dried fish dipped in ",["Seal oil","ingredient/seal-oil"]," is a favorite at any time of year."]],"src":"note","sources":[{"name":"Alaska Native Heritage Center","url":"https://www.alaskanative.net/"},{"name":"University of Alaska Museum of the North, Alaska Native cultures guide","url":"https://uaf.edu/museum/education/docents/pdfs/NC2-3_Intro_2014.pdf"}],"actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"yup-ik-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yup'ik_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Alaska Native foodways","cuisine/alaska-native-foodways"]]},{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Akutaq","dish/akutaq"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Animal fats and oils","ingredient/animal-fats-and-oils"],["Seal oil","ingredient/seal-oil"],["Berry","ingredient/berry"],["Whitefish","ingredient/whitefish"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Alaska Native foodways","cuisine/alaska-native-foodways"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/9/93/Smoked_Salmon.jpg","w":500,"h":484,"by":"GEMalone","lic":"CC BY 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Smoked_Salmon.jpg"}},"cuisine/mordovian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/mordovian-cuisine","n":"Mordovian cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Russia","intro":[["Mordovian cuisine is the traditional cuisine of the Mordovians, who now live in Mordovia and surrounding areas. It consists of a variety of dishes, based on geographical, cultural and climate features of the region, with fish traditionally featured heavily."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"mordovian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mordovian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/9/9a/Erzya_kaymak_%28panzhakay%29_05.jpg/500px-Erzya_kaymak_%28panzhakay%29_05.jpg","w":500,"h":281,"by":"Vaija","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Erzya_kaymak_(panzhakay)_05.jpg"}},"cuisine/danish-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/danish-cuisine","n":"Danish cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Denmark","intro":[["Danish cuisine originated from the peasant population's own local produce and was enhanced by cooking techniques developed in the late 19th century and the wider availability of goods during and after the Industrial Revolution. Open sandwiches, known as smørrebrød, which in their basic form are the usual fare for lunch, can be considered a national speciality when prepared and garnished with a variety of ingredients. Hot meals are typically prepared with meat or fish. Substantial meat and fish dishes includes flæskesteg (roast pork with crackling) and kogt torsk (poached cod) with mustard sauce and trimmings. Ground meats (pork, veal or beef) became widespread during the industrial revolution and traditional dishes that are still popular include frikadeller (meat balls), karbonader (breaded pork patties) and medisterpølse (fried sausage). Denmark is known for its Carlsberg and Tuborg beers and for its akvavit and bitters, but amongst the Danes themselves imported wine has gained steadily in popularity since the 1960s."],["Cooking in Denmark has always been inspired by foreign and continental practises and the use of imported tropical spices like cinnamon, cardamom, nutmeg and black pepper can be traced to the Danish cuisine of the Middle Ages and some even to the Vikings."],["Since the early 2000s, some Danish chefs have developed the new Danish cuisine, an way of cooking based on high-quality local produce. This new philosophy and cuisine has attracted the attention of, and been celebrated by, the international gourmet community. It has contributed with a considerable number of highly acclaimed restaurants in Copenhagen and the province, with some of them awarded Michelin stars."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"danish-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Danish_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Cook Yourself Happy: The Danish Way","ref":"cookbook/cook-yourself-happy-the-danish-way","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Caroline Fleming","note":"Danish home cooking and family recipes from a Danish author.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9781911127284","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":12,"links":[["Æbleflæsk","dish/blefl-sk"],["Æbleskiver","dish/bleskiver"],["Danish pastry","dish/danish-pastry"],["Glögg","dish/glogg"],["Hvidsten Inn","dish/hvidsten-inn"],["Kokkeriet","dish/kokkeriet"],["Kransekage","dish/kransekage"],["Kringle","dish/kringle"],["Labskaus","dish/labskaus"],["Risalamande","dish/risalamande"],["Rødgrød","dish/r-dgr-d"],["Smørrebrød","dish/sm-rrebr-d"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Almond","ingredient/almond"],["Cream","ingredient/cream"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Apple","ingredient/apple"],["Bacon","ingredient/bacon"],["Cinnamon","ingredient/cinnamon"],["Peppercorn","ingredient/peppercorn"],["Yellow onion","ingredient/yellow-onion"],["Buttermilk","ingredient/buttermilk"],["Yeast","ingredient/yeast"],["Mulled wine","ingredient/mulled-wine"],["Raisin","ingredient/raisin"],["Wine","ingredient/wine"],["Egg white","ingredient/egg-white"],["Prunus dulcis","ingredient/prunus-dulcis"],["Rye flour","ingredient/rye-flour"],["Corned beef","ingredient/corned-beef"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Potatoes","ingredient/potatoes"],["Salted meat","ingredient/salted-meat"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Within Danish cooking","count":2,"links":[["Faroese cuisine","cuisine/faroese-cuisine"],["New Nordic cuisine","cuisine/new-nordic-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/3/3e/Flickr_-_cyclonebill_-_Frikadeller_med_ristet_rugbr%C3%B8d_og_cornichons.jpg/500px-Flickr_-_cyclonebill_-_Frikadeller_med_ristet_rugbr%C3%B8d_og_cornichons.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"cyclonebill","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flickr_-_cyclonebill_-_Frikadeller_med_ristet_rugbr%C3%B8d_og_cornichons.jpg"}},"cuisine/mennonite-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/mennonite-cuisine","n":"Mennonite cuisine","c":"#a99fb5","crumb":[["Christian diet","cuisine/christian-dietary-laws"]],"sub":"Religious cuisine or diet","intro":[["Mennonite cuisine is food associated with Mennonites, members of Christian denominations that came out of the Anabaptist movement in sixteenth-century Switzerland and Netherlands. Because of persecution and discrimination against their close-knit communities, they migrated to Prussia, Russia, Poland and the Americas. Groups like the Russian Mennonites developed a sense of ethnicity, which included cuisine adapted from the countries where they lived. Thus, the term Mennonite cuisine does not apply to all, or even most, Mennonites today, especially those outside of the traditional ethnic Mennonite groups. Nor is the food necessarily unique to Mennonites, most of the dishes being variations on recipes common to the countries (Netherlands, Poland, Ukraine, Russia, Latin America) where they reside or resided in the past."],["Mennonites do not have any customary dietary restrictions as exist in some other religious groups. Some conservative Mennonites abstain from alcohol, but other Mennonites do not, with Mennonite distilleries existing as early as the late 16th century."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mennonite_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Christian diet","cuisine/christian-dietary-laws"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"More-with-Less Cookbook","ref":"cookbook/more-with-less-cookbook","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Doris Janzen Longacre, 1976","note":"Herald Press cookbook of recipes gathered from Mennonite cooks on eating simply and frugally; a 40th anniversary edition followed in 2016.","url":"","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Christian diet","cuisine/christian-dietary-laws"]]}],"cont":null,"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/2/27/Farmersausage.jpg/500px-Farmersausage.jpg","w":500,"h":292,"by":"Mennowiki","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Farmersausage.jpg"}},"dish/brix-tavern":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/brix-tavern","n":"Brix Tavern","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from American cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Brix Tavern is a small chain of restaurants in the Portland metropolitan area, in the U.S. state of Oregon. The flagship restaurant operates in northwest Portland's Pearl District and additional locations are in Hillsboro and Tualatin. Previously, the business also operated in Keizer."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-brix-tavern"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brix_Tavern"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"More from American cuisine","links":[["5-8 Club","dish/5-8-club"],["Altabira City Tavern","dish/altabira-city-tavern"],["Angel food cake","dish/angel-food-cake"],["Applebee's","dish/applebee-s"],["Arby's","dish/arby-s"],["Arnold's Bar and Grill","dish/arnold-s-bar-and-grill"],["Banana bread","dish/banana-bread"],["Banana pudding","dish/banana-pudding"],["Barbecue sauce","dish/barbecue-sauce"],["Be Our Guest Restaurant","dish/be-our-guest-restaurant"],["Beer cake","dish/beer-cake"],["Ben's Chili Bowl","dish/ben-s-chili-bowl"],["Benjy's","dish/benjy-s"],["Besaw's","dish/besaw-s"],["Better than sex cake","dish/better-than-sex-cake"],["Blackhawk (restaurant)","dish/blackhawk-restaurant"],["Blini","dish/blini"],["Blooming onion","dish/blooming-onion"],["Bobcat Bite","dish/bobcat-bite"],["Boiling Springs Tavern","dish/boiling-springs-tavern"],["Brown Betty (dessert)","dish/brown-betty-dessert"],["Brown Derby","dish/brown-derby"],["Brunswick stew","dish/brunswick-stew"],["Buffalo wing","dish/buffalo-wing"],["Buona","dish/buona"],["Busboys and Poets","dish/busboys-and-poets"],["Butter cake","dish/butter-cake"],["Byways Cafe","dish/byways-cafe"],["Caesar salad","dish/caesar-salad"],["California Grill","dish/california-grill"],["Camp Washington Chili","dish/camp-washington-chili"],["Caramel apple","dish/caramel-apple"],["Carbonara","dish/carbonara"],["Carnation Cafe","dish/carnation-cafe"],["Cheesesteak","dish/cheesesteak"],["Chicago-style hot dog","dish/chicago-style-hot-dog"],["Chicken nugget","dish/chicken-nugget"],["Chiffon cake","d